.TH MASKTEST 1
.SH NAME
masktest \- check the icons/temple sprite masks, and the code that reads them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B masktest
[
.B \-v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Plan 9 image row is laid out from a byte boundary, not from the
rectangle's left edge:
an image whose
.B r.min.x
is not a multiple of 8/depth carries an extra unit per row and starts
its first pixel part way into the first one \- see
.B libdraw/bytesperline.c .
Every mask in
.B /icons/temple
is 1 bit deep and most of them are centred on the sprite's origin, so
most of them have such a rectangle, and both halves of that layout have
been got wrong here before: once in the files, when the extractors wrote
.RI ( width +7)/8
bytes with the first pixel at bit 0, and once in
.IR draw3d (2),
which computed the same wrong length for the buffer it read a mask into.
.PP
.I Masktest
runs three checks over every
.B .bit
and
.B .mask
pair in
.BR /icons/temple ,
in the order the data flows:
.TP
.B open
Both files open at all, and describe the same rectangle.
A mask whose file is shorter than
.I bytesperline
asks for does not open, and a sprite whose mask came back nil used to
draw as its whole bounding box \- a black rectangle.
.TP
.B draw
A 1:1 masked
.I draw
onto a ground the 16-colour palette does not contain changes exactly the
pixels the mask marks, and no others.
Neither side of that comparison is computed by the test: the coverage
comes back from painting white through the same mask, so a row-length
mistake in the test cannot cancel one in what it is testing.
.TP
.B loop
The same sprite through
.IR draw3d (2)'s
general resample \- the path that reads the mask's bytes itself rather
than handing it to
.I draw
\- agrees with that masked draw in every pixel.
The call is a full turn rather than no turn, which is an identity
rotation but is not literally zero, so it misses the native-blit
shortcut and takes the pixel loop.
.PP
.B \-v
prints a line per sprite rather than only the failures.
The last line is
.B PASSED
or
.BR FAILED .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/masktest.dis
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/masktest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR sprite2dtest (1),
.IR vspritetest (1),
.IR draw3dbench (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The
.B loop
check only tests anything if the corpus still contains a mask whose row
is longer than
.RI ( width +7)/8,
so the test asks
.I readpixels
for every mask at that old length and counts the refusals.
That count is printed as
.BR control: ,
and the test fails if it is zero \- which would mean the shape that
broke had left the tree and the check had quietly stopped covering it.
84 of the 229 masks have it today.
.SH BUGS
It needs a display, so it runs under
.I emu\-cocoa
and not
.IR emu\-g ,
though it draws only into memory.
.PP
It checks the resample at an identity rotation only.
That is the case with an oracle; a reader that got the mask's row right
and the resample wrong at thirty degrees would pass here, and
.IR sprite2dtest (1)
is what covers the resample itself.
.PP
Only
.B /icons/temple
is checked.
The masks under
.B /icons
proper are not, and neither are the 64
.B .mesh
files beside these, which are not images.
.PP
It says nothing about whether a mask marks the right pixels \- only that
whatever it marks is what gets painted.
A mask that covered the wrong half of its sprite would pass every check
here.
